# A bare "-" argument silently discards all following arguments in Split

## Summary

In `Split`, the branch handling a lone `-` argument appends it to `p.Args` and then executes a plain `break`, which exits the whole loop. Every argument after the `-` is dropped without error: `tool - foo bar` ends up with `Args` == `["-"]`.

## Location

- File: `split.go`
- Function: `Split`

```go
	// this is same as firstArg == "-"
	if len(firstArg) == 1 {
		p.Args = append(p.Args, firstArg)
		break
	}
```

## Expected

The arguments are retained: `["-", "foo", "bar"]`, matching Go's stdlib `flag`, which treats a bare `-` like an ordinary operand. Flags after the `-` keep working as they do after any other operand.


FILE split.go:
package argv

import "strings"

// Parsed holds the flags and positional operands of one command line.
type Parsed struct {
	Flags []string
	Args  []string
}

// Split separates flags from operands. Operands keep their exact spelling.
func Split(rargs []string) Parsed {
	var p Parsed
	for ; len(rargs) > 0; rargs = rargs[1:] {
		firstArg := strings.TrimSpace(rargs[0])
		if firstArg == "" {
			p.Args = append(p.Args, rargs[0])
			continue
		}
		if firstArg[0] != '-' {
			p.Args = append(p.Args, rargs[0])
			continue
		}
		if firstArg == "--" {
			p.Args = append(p.Args, rargs[1:]...)
			return p
		}
		// this is same as firstArg == "-"
		if len(firstArg) == 1 {
			p.Args = append(p.Args, firstArg)
			break
		}
		p.Flags = append(p.Flags, firstArg)
	}
	return p
}


